00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.260
and after, you get some
really great ideas on how

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.570
you could use location
intelligence to power

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.420
your business and create the
kinds of user experiences

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.110
you've seen Google produce.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:05.090
So let me take you a little bit
back, a trip down memory lane.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.020
And I'm not talking
that long ago.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:16.130
Just a decade ago, which doesn't
feel very long to me anymore.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.980
When my family would
go out to explore,

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.529
go to a place that
was not familiar,

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:24.140
before we embarked on the trip
the first thing my husband did

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.610
was take another trip.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.740
And this time it was to the AAA.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.590
And he'd go to AAA and get
those little Tripticks,

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.620
and those paper maps, and
we'd stick it in the car,

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:38.360
and that's how we went out and
embarked on our explorations.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.850
Around the same time, So we're
talking about a decade ago,

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:42.350
it was actually a
little bit longer,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:48.590
11 years ago, Google set out to
do something really audacious

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.360
with Google Maps.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.850
We decided we wanted to go and
digitize the map for the world.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:59.000
So we'd have to leave paper
behind and actually create

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.400
a digital version of the
map, but we didn't just

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.210
do this for one location
or even a country.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:07.590
Our goal was a lot
more ambitious.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.790
We wanted to map
the entire world.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:16.700
Every street, every surface,
every business, all of it.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.000
And since then, as
you've seen, we've

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.440
not only brought maps
online all around the world,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.520
we've also created these really
beautiful, rich experiences

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.840
where you can experience
the corners of the planet

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.276
from the tops of the highest
mountains to the oceans,

00:05:38.276 --> 00:05:40.150
as I said, without
leaving your seat or even,

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.190
in this case,
learning how to dive.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:48.640
And because increasingly we saw
that this world was becoming

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.330
mobile and connected, we brought
these experiences to mobile.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.880
Essentially what we ended
up giving all the users

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:59.820
in the world with a phone was a
handy guide to the real world.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.780
And this could help them then
get to where they were going,

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.300
provide the best experience
when they arrived,

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.730
and even provide
interesting ideas

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:08.900
for detours along the way.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.840
Now, that sounds so great.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:22.090
11 years later we're very
proud of where we've come,

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.780
but this was not easy.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.300
We had several walls.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.297
These were big hurdles that we
had to climb over the years.

00:06:29.297 --> 00:06:31.630
Because what we were trying
to do was something that was

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:32.560
a one-of-a-kind.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.950
It had never been done before
and certainly not at the scale

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.670
that we were aspiring for.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:42.600
So when we hit some
serious technology hurdles,

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.440
what did we do?

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.210
We're a company of engineers.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:48.070
And what do engineers do
when they find that there

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.630
is a technological hurdle?

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.169
They build new technology.

00:06:53.169 --> 00:06:54.210
That's how they solve it.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.230
They fix it.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:58.470
So essentially what we did,
over the course of our journey

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.290
as we were scaling, was we
built new infrastructure

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:04.680
that would help us get to the
scale that we were aspiring to.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.460
And we also found new ways
and rethought the ways

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:09.900
we would develop our products.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:17.260
Google Cloud and
Machine Learning,

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:22.180
that these two very powerful
pairs, along with Google Maps,

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.470
changed our business.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.690
And that's what we call, within
Google, location intelligence.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:34.930
Now, location
intelligence gave us

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:39.460
within Google the scale, the
power, and the infrastructure

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.010
to help us with our dreams
of knowing everything

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.030
about the real world.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.110
And now what we feel
really excited about

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.800
are the possibilities of
bringing this to your world.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.790
What powered maps,
and local search,

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:56.830
and Earth can be used for
enterprise use cases as well.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.510
But we, I want to go
further than that.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:05.390
I mean, it's great, but we don't
think location intelligence is

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:06.950
just an imperative.

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:09.920
We actually think it's
a force multiplier

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.270
in this mobile connected world.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.110
So what does that mean,
a force multiplier?

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.340
In this case, it means
that every service

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:20.300
that you're building
could be improved

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.080
with location intelligence.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.720
You could elevate your
offering in a way that

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.150
could be disruptive,
disruptive to your business,

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.640
disruptive to the ecosystem.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.090
And build solutions that
have location based insights

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:37.929
and can be delivered at
scale and in real time.

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:40.030
Now, I'll share with
you a few examples

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:43.990
of how enterprises have used
the rich data of Google Maps,

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.530
the scale of Cloud, and the
power of Machine Learning

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:49.900
to grow their businesses.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:53.180
Because it's already
happening today.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.120
So let me start with
Google Maps API.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.460
This is one of our
older products.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.350
We launched this
around the same time

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.370
as we launched our consumer
Google Maps product.

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:07.060
And over the years, as we
continue to trade and improve

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.280
our consumer
product, we continue

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.410
to augment our Maps API.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.390
I think several of
you in this room

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:14.740
are probably familiar with this.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.780
This Is almost a decade old
and constantly advancing.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:25.510
So we see four main use cases in
how customers and partners use

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:27.830
our Maps API.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:32.150
Display, Go,
Locate, and Explore.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:35.600
So what's Display?

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.640
Display is simple.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:44.510
It's when you want to put a
map on your app or website.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:49.170
Go helps users navigate
around the world.

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:53.580
Locate is the use case for
tracking uses or assets

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.120
around the world.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.410
And finally, Explore is
this really rich knowledge

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.630
of places and businesses
around the world

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.660
that Google has captured
that we make available to you

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:06.250
through our Places API.

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:09.460
So let's start with Display.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:13.110
As I said, this is
at the core of Maps.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:17.390
This is when we
use our visual map

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:21.140
and provide this to you so
you can build custom maps that

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:23.690
can be personalized
for your use cases

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.235
and for the needs of your users.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.000
Now, here's a great
example of a customer who's

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:35.660
used Google Maps, the
visualization API,

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:37.460
to build a really
great experience.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:42.020
So they created a tool called
GoodHome, which essentially,

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.880
because they're in the
insurance business,

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.800
they really needed to find a way
to give their users the ability

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.100
to get personalized
quotes so they could start

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:56.120
the process of converting
them from users on a website

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:57.830
to customers.

00:10:57.830 --> 00:10:59.690
And so, with GoodHome
what they did was

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:03.470
they created a
personalized experience

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:06.200
that was personalized for your
locale, for where you were.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.160
So incorporating risk.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.670
So, for example, in a given
neighborhood, what the crime

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.350
rate was in that neighborhood.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:16.820
Or in a region that is swampy,
what the risk of flooding is.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.370
Or if you're around
here, the risk

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:22.850
of the big one hitting you.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:25.610
So what they found, and
this was really impressive,

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.010
they found that
with this localized

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.860
personalized
experience users were

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:34.490
350% more likely
to ask for a quote

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.450
and then get to conversion.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:38.930
And what is also
pretty awesome is

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.990
they were able to
go from concept,

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:45.080
from thinking about
this as a design phase,

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:48.140
to completing and launching
the site in under 90 days.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.900
So this is really powerful
because, ultimately,

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:53.360
the technology that we
were providing really

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:57.230
came to a place where a
customer at the end of the day

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:01.600
was able to get a
better experience.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:05.250
Let's take the other, the
next use case, navigation.

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.120
What we found is, over
the years customers

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.060
have used our advanced
navigation capabilities

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.430
to really transform businesses.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.340
And as you're seeing, I
think in recent years,

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.850
disrupt whole industries.

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:22.130
A great example of this is Lyft.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:25.810
They're a great customer and
they use our real time location

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:28.420
data to connect
drivers and riders.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:33.340
If any of you have used
a Lyft, Lyft or an Uber,

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:36.670
I think you'll see that that
whole experience around, right

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:40.060
from when you find where
your driver is as he's coming

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.940
to you, through your
ride experience,

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:46.090
and as well as the driver as
he is making his way to get you

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:50.830
to your destination, all that
is powered through our Maps

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:52.870
navigation APIs.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.990
And what really has happened
over the last very few years

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:59.500
actually, is this
technology has actually

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.790
created an entirely
new business category,

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:06.340
something that we didn't
exist a few years ago.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:09.880
And they've had some phenomenal
successes, in the case of Lyft.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:11.990
There were two
million Lyft riders

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:17.750
monthly with 100,000 drivers
on payroll in 200 cities.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:19.720
So I came in a Lyft
today to the Moscone.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.400
Previously, I'd come
to San Francisco

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:24.880
and I'd worry about parking
and all I do now is book a ride

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:26.840
and I'm here.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:29.830
So what's really interesting
about this use case is,

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:31.900
if you think about
it, Google set out

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:34.590
to understand how
the world moved

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:38.320
and how users moved
around the world.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:40.480
And essentially now, what
our customers have done

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:43.000
is they've used our
technology and our products

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.970
to change the way users move.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:48.090
So I think that's really cool.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:51.124
The third use case is
closely tied to navigation,

00:13:51.124 --> 00:13:52.540
but this is really
around tracking

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:56.500
of vehicles, people, assets,
even connected devices

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.850
with IoT around the world.

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:02.500
Now, location tracking makes
supply chains very efficient

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:06.580
and logistics a lot
more competitive.

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:10.660
In this new world where it
is about on-demand real time

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:14.350
delivery, these are
now table stakes.

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:16.540
And here's a customer that's
built something that's

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:19.810
really cool using our APIs.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:25.000
So DPD is a customer that uses
our live tracking, basically

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:31.450
our location APIs, to deliver
3.6 million parcels daily.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.360
And as they move to our APIs
what they were able to do

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.900
was to introduce live
tracking of those parcels.

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:41.380
So, in this case, what you
can do with DPD is the sender

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:44.350
and the recipient can both
look at the exact position

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:47.230
of a package through
its transit following

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.270
the journey on a
map and the arrival

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:54.250
ETA is within 30 minutes of
the projected delivery time.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:58.150
Now, a few years
ago, if you remember,

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:00.580
we'd know we'd get this
on a Thursday maybe

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.220
and sometimes a package would
come in on a Friday instead.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.790
And here we are now, tracking
these things in real time,

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:09.250
expecting to track
them in real time.

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:13.330
And even, in my case because I
travel a lot, from time to time

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.010
I'll redirect the
packages when I know

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.920
that I'm not going to be home.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:21.910
This transparency, this
control, this level of service

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:25.480
that was just not possible
before, are table stakes now.

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:27.160
Customers demand it.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.260
And the technology
that we've provided

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:35.380
that our customers are now using
essentially make this possible.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.750
And finally, the last
use cases around Explore.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:41.520
Now everyone uses Google,
Google's local search.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.990
And what we really thought
was really important to do

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:46.999
was to provide.

00:15:46.999 --> 00:15:48.540
Because we know that
ultimately there

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.090
are users who come to your
products and your services

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.980
that don't come to Google's
local search, not always.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:55.740
That we wanted to
make sure that you

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.380
had access to the same
rich knowledge of places

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:02.040
around the world
through our Maps APIs.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.400
So here's an example of a
customer that's done exactly

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:06.700
that.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:09.720
The travel aggregator site
called Tripping, what they do

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:12.720
is they use our
Maps API for Places

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:15.660
to get address listings
and destination information

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:17.530
for their rental properties.

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:19.890
So what they do is they take
the location information

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:23.970
from each listing that they
have and they drop it on a map.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:26.730
And because of the way
we've made this work

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.820
it automatically scales the
map to match your query.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:35.010
So, for example, if you're
searching for rentals in Paris

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:36.840
it'll map it to the
correct zoom level.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.660
And if you decide you want to
get a little bit more local

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:43.031
and you want to look at
neighborhoods around the Eiffel

00:16:43.031 --> 00:16:43.530
Tower.

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.779
I don't know why you would
because it's really crowded

00:16:45.779 --> 00:16:48.900
around there, but if you did
you'd get to that tighter zoom

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:55.720
almost immediately without
users having to pinch, and pan,

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:56.740
and zoom.

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.420
So it's all
beautifully integrated

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.230
in the consumer experience.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:04.900
The other thing that Tripping in
particular found really useful

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:07.390
was just having
accurate address data.

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:10.300
So what they found was that
the rental property owners

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:13.089
didn't always provide
the full address,

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:19.930
making it really hard
for potential renters

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:21.484
to get to the place.

00:17:21.484 --> 00:17:22.900
But that was not
a problem anymore

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.869
because with Google's
Maps API we essentially,

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:28.540
and because of our knowledge of
the addresses around the world,

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:30.100
we could complete it
for you, essentially.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.516
So if we see an
address that's not

00:17:31.516 --> 00:17:35.080
complete we can match it with
the address in our records,

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:38.180
essentially fill in the blanks.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:42.166
So what's really awesome about
this case was the numbers.

00:17:42.166 --> 00:17:43.666
When I first saw
the slide I thought

00:17:43.666 --> 00:17:46.060
it was 200% increase, which
seemed really awesome,

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:52.170
but this was closer to 2400%.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:55.830
And they got two million
users very quickly after they

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:58.350
launched this new site.

00:17:58.350 --> 00:17:59.885
Now this is not a big company.

00:17:59.885 --> 00:18:02.010
You probably have not all
heard of Tripping the way

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.150
you may have heard of Lyft.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.600
But as a result
of this they were

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.970
able to recently
close a series B round

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.950
and get more venture funding.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:14.760
And I say this
because it's examples

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:18.720
like this that get us
really excited within Google

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:20.580
because they're exciting
to see the birth

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:24.600
of these new companies that
are able to use technology

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:27.900
to drive these really
great user experiences

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:29.130
and create new businesses.

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:35.530
For our customers
that use our tools

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.980
and provide great experiences,
now this is really exciting.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:40.480
We think in every
one of these cases

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.980
they've done something
very special.

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:47.780
But there's another class
of emerging use cases

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.450
that operate at massive scale.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:51.980
And these are new.

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:54.630
These are happening but
there are still emerging,

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.990
and they are made possible
by the computation

00:18:56.990 --> 00:19:00.860
scale of Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:04.040
You're here for a reason
because of Google Cloud.

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:08.150
And I want to tell you that
when you think about Maps,

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:11.780
Google Cloud is a great pair.

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:15.080
And here are a few examples
of why we think that is so.

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:24.760
So prime example here of this
really magical powerhouse

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:28.240
pairing of Google
Cloud and Google Maps

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:29.920
is a product called
Earth Engine.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:31.940
This was developed at Google.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:35.260
And what we did here was
we turned Google Cloud's

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:40.660
enormous computing horsepower on
this rich world of geo datasets

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:42.520
that we have.

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:45.670
And what we were able to
do was to enable insights

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:48.140
at unprecedented speeds.

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:50.690
Now, the first application
of Earth Engine

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:51.710
was not commercial.

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:55.610
It was actually about mapping
the forests of the Earth.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:58.110
It's just, it's a beautiful
thing because ultimately,

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.620
if this is about
the Earth, our trees

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:02.420
are an incredible
natural resource.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.970
And having knowledge,
and access,

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:11.510
and understanding how our
forest ecosystem is doing

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:14.910
is actually a really important
part of our mission as well.

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:18.290
But there had never
been, previously,

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:23.270
a way to systematically
map changes in the Earth,

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:26.212
map it to forest areas
at a high resolution.

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.610
And that's what we did.

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:36.910
We partnered with Google Forest
Watch and Professor Hansen

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:40.480
and created the world's
first high resolution

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:42.460
global forests change map.

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:45.400
So those of you, I hope
you were able to see

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:49.810
that this map got successful
successively pinker as I

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:51.220
was speaking.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:54.130
That was basically what
you could see happen in

00:20:54.130 --> 00:21:01.510
that very few seconds was really
a recording of the changes

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.870
in deforestation within the US.

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:08.320
So the more pink you see
the worse the news is.

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:10.150
And what we're also
able to do is not only

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:12.160
track the status of
these forest losses,

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.530
we can also provide
alerts on when

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:19.330
there are recent forest losses
so we can keep this updated.

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:21.850
Now, to create
this global forest

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:25.060
map we had to process,
this was not trivial,

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:29.740
we had a process 650,000
images across 10,000

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:33.200
Cloud-based servers in parallel.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:37.610
Now, this would take a
single computer 15 years.

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:41.247
That's even well before the
time we created Google Maps.

00:21:41.247 --> 00:21:42.830
And we were able to
do this with Earth

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:45.156
Engine in just a few days.

00:21:45.156 --> 00:21:47.780
Now, if we could only figure out
how to regrow those trees that

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:50.540
fast, but these are
some of the examples

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:55.400
you can see of just the
power with Cloud, and Maps,

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:59.360
and the applications
that can be provided.

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:01.610
Something we tried at Google,
but we also have another

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:02.680
example here. .

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.180
And this one's very
different So I'm

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:07.100
taking you from the forest
to the world of Poke stops.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:09.650
So here we have
Niantic and Pokemon Go.

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:13.190
Who here hasn't
played Pokemon Go?

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:14.120
Wow.

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:16.120
OK.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:20.620
I should help you download
the app as you leave.

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:22.640
I'm not a gamer, but
I spent, I would say,

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:26.570
at least a week last
summer playing the game

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:28.970
and really discovering
my neighborhood.

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:30.650
I had not walked
many of these streets

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:35.000
so it was a very unusual, a
very innovative experience.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:36.920
A completely new
gaming experience

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:40.340
that starts to give you
glimpses of the world we

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:43.190
see in the future of
augmented reality experiences.

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:45.530
And what they did was
they basically leveraged

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:50.180
a custom version of our Maps
API, among other sources,

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:53.250
to create this gaming
experience, which is great.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:54.530
We loved it.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:58.129
But there was another really
interesting angle here.

00:22:58.129 --> 00:23:00.170
In addition to creating
this really unique gaming

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:04.710
experience, they
found themselves,

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:07.875
as could be evidenced in
just the number of hands

00:23:07.875 --> 00:23:09.250
in this room,
there were only two

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:12.090
so you can imagine if
we extrapolate this

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:16.140
around the world, they
found themselves caught up

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:19.650
in this unexpected
explosive growth.

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:23.820
Now, growth is always a
good thing for a company.

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:26.140
2x 3x, you think you can handle.

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:26.640
They.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.630
Went up to 50x in
that first week.

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:31.480
That's meltdown time.

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:34.710
But in this case, both
Niantic and the engineers

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:37.260
from Google Cloud were able
to work together and help

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:39.000
them scale.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:40.500
And this is just the beginning.

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:43.890
As I said, this is
the first glimpse

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:45.690
of what we believe
is really going

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.090
to be some of the
experiences we see more

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:50.280
of in the future,
where companies

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:52.740
start to create these
augmented reality experiences.

00:23:52.740 --> 00:23:57.720
Where they merge the
world with other artifacts

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:00.810
and create these really
incredible augmented reality

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:02.220
experiences.

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.740
I hope these two
examples gave you

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:09.390
a sense, a glimpse, of just, the
power and the capabilities when

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:13.380
you pair rich real world
data from Maps with the.

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.255
Computing power
and scale of GCP.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.690
So going back again a
little bit to our journey,

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:28.870
because we found that
every time we go,

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:32.810
we rewind and look at where
we are, we found, as I said,

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:35.560
we kept finding ourselves
hitting roadblocks.

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:39.600
So in the early days
in order to create

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:43.230
the digital map
for Google Maps we

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:46.400
had a team of engineers,
not thousands.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:48.870
Google always runs really lean.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.600
We had a sizable
team of programmers

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:56.070
that were building these
applications with hand coded

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:56.970
rules.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:57.720
And this was fine.

00:24:57.720 --> 00:24:58.440
This was great.

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:00.310
It was working.

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:02.780
Until it wasn't.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:05.900
As we started to build
richer experiences

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:09.570
and we raised the bar on
what it meant to be a map,

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.410
it was not just a digital
version of a paper map.

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:14.270
It was a map that had
to live and breathe.

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.100
It had to be in real time.

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.070
We wanted rich experiences
that could even

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:19.820
give you some prediction
about the future.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:25.590
We found ourselves in a position
where our old development

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:27.840
strategies just wouldn't scale.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:29.760
We couldn't have gone
from 100 to 1,000.

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:31.710
We would have had to
have tens of thousands

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:34.590
of engineers to do
that, to basically meet

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:37.680
the demands of our dreams.

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:42.060
So that's when we turned
to Machine Learning.

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:43.620
With Machine Learning
we essentially

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:46.740
gave computers the ability
to learn from the data

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.890
and make predictions
on the data.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:54.900
So a great example of Machine
Learning on Google Maps

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:59.490
is a feature we launched very
recently called Popular Times.

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:04.140
So here we're able to give you
an indication for any place

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:07.710
that you want to visit, what
might be the popular times

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:08.670
of the less busy times.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:12.720
So you can decide how you
want to schedule your visit.

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:15.300
And we did this with
Machine Learning.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:21.960
Another great example is the
map you see on the right.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:26.820
So here the reds indicate
high traffic and the greens

00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:30.200
are roads that don't
have much traffic.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:35.570
Now, our Machine Learning models
predicted these traffic delays

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:39.440
in Wyoming based on
weather changes ahead of us

00:26:39.440 --> 00:26:40.850
even getting the data feeds.

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:44.300
So we knew what was
going to happen.

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:47.810
Not we, our Machine
Learning models knew.

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:50.630
And this notion of having
predictive real time

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:52.700
experiences just
simply would not

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:56.350
have been possible if we hadn't
invested in Machine Learning.

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:03.510
So with Machine Learning,
and I think there are many,

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:06.050
many talks here over
the next coming days.

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:09.170
I would really ask you to
go spend some time there

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:11.810
because this is fundamentally
going to change the way we

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:15.150
think about almost everything.

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:16.040
But back to our case.

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:17.930
What we've found is
we can use the signals

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:21.590
from connected devices, from
real time maps, and location

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:24.260
data, and answer new
questions and make

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:27.810
really powerful predictions
about the real world.

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:31.490
But what is equally exciting
is that this deep knowledge

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:34.100
that we have now, this
understanding of location,

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:36.920
and Machine Learning, and
Cloud computing, we now

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:40.700
have the potential to
help you, enterprises,

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:42.680
build the smart
assistive solutions

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:45.580
that we've been trying to
release on our own Google

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:47.410
surfaces.

00:27:47.410 --> 00:27:50.109
So here's a great example.

00:27:50.109 --> 00:27:51.400
It's a really powerful example.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:53.325
It's also a deeply
humbling example.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:28:00.490
So I'm going to take you from
the forests now to the oceans.

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.250
The health of our planet's
oceans, I think you all know,

00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:06.730
are under threat from
global overfishing.

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:11.170
And what's great though, is
some of these smart assistive

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:13.930
solutions that I was
talking about are now

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:17.510
being used to monitor, prevent,
and address these situations.

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:21.640
So here's how this is happening
in this particular case.

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:25.330
There are about 200,000
vessels around the world,

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:29.080
shipping vessels, that broadcast
their location, their course,

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:32.950
their speed back to satellites
up in the skies, fleets

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:36.840
of satellites that record
these streams and then transmit

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:39.160
the information back to Earth.

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:41.860
Now, we took this
information in partnership

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:45.310
with the Global Fishing
Watch organization

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:48.670
and using Google's Cloud
computing and our Machine

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:54.100
Learning models, we were able
to pinpoint which of the vessels

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:58.720
there were fishing vessels, when
and where they were fishing,

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:01.450
and also be able to make an
assessment of whether there

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:05.320
